::PC_U04_L03_Q01::
Question ID: PC_U04_L03_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.TR.3
Question:
Which identity comes from the unit circle?
{
~A. sin(x)+cos(x)=1#This choice does not match the required identity or formula.
=B. sin^2(x)+cos^2(x)=1#Correct. The unit circle relationship gives sin^2(x)+cos^2(x)=1.
~C. tan(x)=cos(x)/sin(x)#This reflects a common misconception; write the identity before substituting.
~D. sec(x)=sin(x)#This answer does not preserve the trigonometric relationship from the lesson.
}
::PC_U04_L03_Q02::
Question ID: PC_U04_L03_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.TR.3
Question:
If cos(x)=12/13 in Quadrant I, what is sin(x)?
{
~A. 12/13#This choice does not match the required identity or formula.
~B. 144/169#This reflects a common misconception; write the identity before substituting.
~C. 25/169#This answer does not preserve the trigonometric relationship from the lesson.
=D. 5/13#Correct. sin^2(x)=1-144/169=25/169, so sin(x)=5/13.
}
::PC_U04_L03_Q03::
Question ID: PC_U04_L03_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.TR.3
Question:
Which identity is equivalent to 1+cot^2(x)=csc^2(x)?
{
=A. csc^2(x)-cot^2(x)=1#Correct. Subtracting cot^2(x) from both sides gives csc^2(x)-cot^2(x)=1.
~B. tan^2(x)+1=sin^2(x)#This choice does not match the required identity or formula.
~C. sin^2(x)+cos^2(x)=2#This reflects a common misconception; write the identity before substituting.
~D. sec(x)-tan(x)=1#This answer does not preserve the trigonometric relationship from the lesson.
}
::PC_U04_L03_Q04::
Question ID: PC_U04_L03_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.TR.3
Question:
Why is quadrant information important when finding a ratio?
{
~A. It changes the identity into a formula#This choice does not match the required identity or formula.
~B. It removes the need to square values#This reflects a common misconception; write the identity before substituting.
=C. It determines whether the final ratio is positive or negative#Correct. Squaring removes sign, so quadrant information restores the correct sign.
~D. It makes every ratio positive#This answer does not preserve the trigonometric relationship from the lesson.
}
::PC_U04_L03_Q05::
Question ID: PC_U04_L03_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.TR.3
Question:
Which statement best describes an identity proof?
{
~A. It proves a statement by testing one angle#This choice does not match the required identity or formula.
~B. It changes only the answer choices#This reflects a common misconception; write the identity before substituting.
=C. It uses known identities to create equivalent expressions#Correct. Identity proofs use known relationships and equivalent transformations.
~D. It avoids algebraic reasoning#This answer does not preserve the trigonometric relationship from the lesson.
}
